------\nTOTAL                                                                      4581    817    82%\n\u001b[32m============================= \u001b[32m\u001b[1m659 passed\u001b[0m\u001b[32m in 13.36s\u001b[0m\u001b[32m =============================\u001b[0m","passed":true,"required":true}],"last_task_run_event_id":"evt_8898d985fcce7a278445c0f1","objective":"Close the specific CreateDirectoryTool proof gap carried from cycle 4, starting from cd0817e22def141c8d9708cc1a2ddec263c773cb. Add assertion-bearing regression coverage through CreateDirectoryTool.run() with a real FilesystemGateway and filesystem path boundary, without mocking private methods or library internals. Demonstrate that the same unexpected-error assertion rejects c95d805e617f521c77cc04866b2232c11d0baba8 because its catch-all converts the failure into a string, and passes against the corrected source with the original typed failure preserved and no unintended filesystem mutation. Characterize successful directory creation and retained expected-error behavior on both revisions. This documents the exception-propagation correction licensed by Stacey’s October 10 lint-cleanup authorization; do not introduce additional runtime changes. Preserve the constructor’s direct FilesystemGateway contract and the coding_file_tool example’s usage. Record early proof in .foundry/proof.json, retain complete rejecting/passing captures, exact revisions, source hashes and evidence hashes durably outside disposable worktrees, and update RECOVERY-CONFORMANCE.md and migration notes without claiming historical chronology or whole-mission completion. Obtain independent review of the assertions and compatibility evidence. Refresh the actual final-source project gates: full Ruff and formatting, full and required selective Flake8, pytest and explicit coverage, MkDocs, Bandit, both unfiltered dependency audits, and the outdated-package check. Verify both audit launchers inspect the project environment. Use foundry capture for verbose checks. Preserve rules, thresholds, runtime pins, exclusions, dependencies, active suppressions and all existing recovery assertions. Inspect dirty state and controller synchronization evidence; worker ref mutations remain prohibited. Deliver this focused change for controller landing directly on main, without a PR. No cleanup rebuild, provider expansion, sibling/harness edits, release, live inference or experiment restart.","project":"mojentic-py","prompt":"You are advancing a durable engineering campaign. Inspect the repository yourself; descriptive metadata is never current state. Decide exactly one of done, advance, or escalate. You return a decision; the daemon dispatches it. Never invoke foundry task/campaign, probe localhost, or infer daemon availability from this sandbox. WRITABLE REPOSITORY: this project only; sibling repositories are read-only.\n\nCAMPAIGN: mojentic-py-transient-recovery-v2\nMISSION: Implement and document the complete transient completion recovery contract in the Python Mojentic library, with deterministic public-entrypoint conformance tests for all applicable completion adapters including Ollama and oMLX. Normative scope is TRANSIENT-RECOVERY-2026-10.md and the original RECOVERY-REQUEST-2026-10.txt. Preserve retries-disabled compatibility and immutable semantic requests; expose safe structured errors, bounded jitter/Retry-After recovery, cancellable async admission, attempt identity/history, explicit stream interruption after semantic delivery, truthful unsupported capabilities, privacy-safe lifecycle events and opt-in per-wire exact trace hooks. No tool replay, no overlapping ambiguous local inference without explicit admission or exact termination evidence, no total generation timeout, no live-model requests, no benchmark restart, no release or dependency upgrade. The owner authorizes this new feature across all six ports; old 2.1.0 release-only notes are historical and do not limit this request. Changes land directly on main, without PRs. Sync fetch and pull --rebase origin main before coding; investigate dirty state and surface conflicts. Run all required gates and applicable audits without lowering standards. Add migration examples and capability notes. You are not alone; do not revert others work. Repository scope is this port only; sibling ports and the harness are read-only. Follow the Elixir reference behavior and copied reference API notes where the contract is silent; implement an idiomatic equivalent, not a private helper disconnected from public completion paths.\n\nOWNER AUTHORIZATION OCTOBER 10, 2026: Stacey directs closing superseded obligations and bringing every other port up to the reviewed Rust capabilities. The following supplement is binding. Existing Elixir behavior must not override the corrected safety/telemetry requirements.\n\n# Recovery alignment with the reviewed Rust implementation\n\nStacey authorized this work on October 10, 2026. Bring the other five ports\nup to the capabilities verified in Rust. Elixir remains the API reference.\nThe original recovery contract remains binding. This supplement adds the\nbehavior proved during Rust and harness integration. Do not copy known gaps.\n\n## Reviewed evidence\n\nThe reviewed Rust implementation is\n`4ca1ed279c02eab37827a1ed07c30e961155ecf3`.\nRead its recovery modules, public tests, migration guide, and conformance report.\nUse the exact revision, rather than an unreviewed working tree.\nThe ops checkout is\n`/home/svetzal/Work/Projects/Personal/mojentic-unify/mojentic-ru`.\nSibling repositories are read-only.\n\nThe harness integration is `8739f6dadc24df9264d8a909aac27df72d23f2be`.\nCurrent harness main is `a44bae60a71cea999ce1c79ce6e588912d98457c`.\nRequired harness checks passed, with 357 tests and two existing exclusions.\nThree scripted repetitions passed 306 provider cases and 21 configuration refusals.\nAll six controlled live cells passed on Ollama and oMLX. Completed tools ran once.\nThese results prove controlled recovery mechanics. Natural recovery efficacy and\nsix-port parity remain unproved. Do not restart any stopped experiment.\n\nHistorical Rust conformance sections describe earlier provisional worktrees.\nThe ledger confirms the final revision was reviewed and landed. Preserve the\nhistorical failures. Do not interpret their old finalization text as current state.\n\n## Added acceptance requirements\n\nImplement ordinary, structured, and streaming request recovery through public\nadapters. Exercise public broker and session paths where they exist.\nUse idiomatic APIs. Preserve legacy behavior when recovery is disabled.\nRetries remain opt-in, with one attempt by default. Keep attempt accounting\nseparate from logical completions, model interactions, and tool depth.\n\nEncode the semantic request once. Keep its bytes unchanged across retries.\nInclude messages, supported native reasoning history, tools, schemas, and controls.\nDisable hidden transport retries and redirects on recovery-enabled requests.\nPreserve the current supported reasoning behavior. Do not add unrelated features.\n\nRetain numeric HTTP status, headers, received bytes, original causes, and history.\nKeep safe metadata separate from explicit inspection and raw capture.\nCapture hooks are caller-owned and opt-in. Document their sensitive content.\nSafe errors, events, formatting, and serialization must omit credential echoes.\nTest metadata values that echo outbound credentials or payloads.\n\nA truncated error body must not override a known permanent HTTP status.\nHTTP 400, 401, and 403 remain ineligible even if transport eligibility is enabled.\nTest all applicable operations and adapters, including a selected permanent status.\nKeep the original body-read cause and partial response evidence privately inspectable.\n\nAccount for received semantic evidence before a body capture hook runs.\nA failed capture hook is terminal and cannot cause another request.\nRetain observed reasoning, content, and tool fragments with zero delivered progress\nwhen the hook fails before delivery. Preserve typed causes through broker/session errors.\n\nCancellation is authoritative in active requests, admission, backoff, and terminal frames.\nCheck cancellation before successful completion, including terminal-only stream chunks.\nRecord the failed actual attempt before one terminal cancellation event.\nDo not emit success after cancellation. Preserve available headers and progress.\nAn idle consumer must not prevent cancellation from closing locally owned resources.\nTest cancellation with buffered terminal telemetry and paused consumers.\nLocal socket closure is not proof of remote inference termination.\n\nPreserve provider progress and metrics already exposed by the port's normal stream.\nRecovery wrappers must retain their values, order, and attempt identity.\nInclude UTF-8 byte counts, frame indices, durations, and token counts where available.\nReset per-attempt frame indices. Metadata is not semantic output or a new interaction.\nDo not invent counters on providers that do not supply them.\n\nA valid Ollama length-terminated frame can contain useful telemetry and still fail.\nDeliver validated progress and metrics before the original terminal failure.\nTest exact values and Progress, Metrics, Failed ordering at the HTTP boundary.\nPrevent completed-tool delivery, replay, and successful completion in this case.\nMalformed frames must not produce invented telemetry.\nDo not change unrelated ordinary generation finish behavior.\n\nRetain the full original acceptance matrix. This includes Retry-After, bounded 504,\nambiguous admission, all semantic interruption channels, keepalive, and tool safety.\nAn observed partial semantic response blocks transparent replay, even before delivery.\nA keepalive alone does not establish remote termination or safe admission.\nRequire exact request ownership evidence or explicit caller admission for local retries.\nDo not add a wall-clock timeout to healthy generation.\n\n## Proof and delivery\n\nRecord early public-boundary proof in `.foundry/proof.json` before broad expansion.\nUse scripted loopback HTTP and synthetic fixture values. Never call a real model.\nTest every maintained applicable completion adapter, not just a private helper.\nAssert exact payload bytes, IDs, causes, progress, lifecycle order, and wire counts.\nTest a completed tool followed by a failed completion. The tool must execute once.\nUse deterministic clocks, sleepers, jitter, and cancellation synchronization.\nKeep rejecting cases and failed fixture runs distinct from production defects.\n\nRetain complete logs and proof outside temporary worktrees before cleanup.\nUse `foundry capture` for verbose checks. Record hashes and exact source revisions.\nRun every required project gate and applicable unfiltered security audit.\nKeep coverage floors, runtime pins, gate scope, and existing exclusions unchanged.\nUpdate API snapshots only for the intended additive public API.\nDo not hide security findings or fold dependency upgrades into this feature.\n\nUpdate `RECOVERY-CONFORMANCE.md`, migration examples, and provider capability tables.\nState remaining limits honestly. A test name alone is not acceptance evidence.\nRequire independent whole-mission review before calling the port aligned.\nDo not publish packages or claim coordinated parity during this implementation work.\n\n## Repository and host boundaries\n\nInspect dirty state before edits. Fetch and pull with rebase on main.\nStop on a rebase conflict. Do not stash or revert another session's work.\nLand focused commits directly on main. Do not create PRs.\nOnly the campaign's own port is writable. The harness and other ports are read-only.\nThis authorization supersedes the historical September release-only restriction.\nIt does not authorize unrelated dependency changes, releases, or live inference.\n\nProduction Foundry runs all five campaigns on ops-01 with explicit Cod